# Frontend Proxy And Auth Consistency

- Browser-side requests should use the Next.js `/api/*` proxy or a shared frontend API helper.
- Do not call `${API_BASE_URL}` directly from pages, layouts, or components unless a documented blob/download helper truly requires it.
- Remove localhost-only telemetry, debug fetches, and ad hoc network calls from normal runtime code.
- Reuse shared auth and provider patterns; do not add overlapping business/session/config providers for the same route tree.
- Protected route trees should have one clear contract: server-side gate, shared layout guard, or explicit forbidden state. Avoid page-by-page signed-out behavior drift.

```typescript
// ✅ preferred
await fetch('/api/profile', { headers: authHeaders(token) });

// ❌ drift-prone
await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}/profile`, { headers: authHeaders(token) });
```

- If a direct backend export/download path is unavoidable, centralize it in one helper and document why it cannot use the proxy.
